  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nNortheast Montana will remain in NW flow aloft the next few days\nbetween an upper low over Hudson Bay and northern Manitoba and an\nupper ridge over the Rockies and Pacific NW. A dry cold front is\nexpected to move in from the north today which will bring gusty NW\nwinds up on Fort Peck Lake, Nelson Reservoir, and Medicine Lake,\nand will create critical fire weather conditions south of the\nMissouri River. Will keep Lake Wind Advisory in effect for Fort\nPeck Lake and Red Flag Warning in effect south of the Missouri\nRiver this afternoon and evening.\n\nSmoke from wildfires out west will move east into the forecast\narea this morning. However, the cold front will push the smoke to\nthe south this afternoon limiting its impact on air quality. \n\nFriday will be cooler in the north and east while the southwest \nwill have temperatures similar to Thursday being near a quasi- \nstationary front.  \n\nThe GFS and ECMWF ensembles are suggesting a flattening of the\nridge this weekend as it moves through eastern Montana. At the\nsame time, a frontal boundary will remain quasi-stationary along\nthe southern part of the forecast area so that the north will have\nhighs mainly in the 80s while the south has highs mainly in the\n90s. \n\nOn Sunday, there is a wave moving across Southern Saskatchewan\nwhich could bring a chance of showers mainly along the Canadian\nBorder. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nThe only deviation was to increase winds behind the cold front\nthis afternoon. \n\nThere is high confidence in the Lake Wind Advisory today.\n\nThere is low confidence of isolated smoke and haze reducing \nvisibility slightly around midday today.\n\nThere is high confidence in critical fire weather occurring \nthis afternoon south of the Missouri River.",
